## Introduction
Stimuli responsive fluorescent materials have been explored extensively in the past decades.[1] Among which, pressureinduced fluorochromic material represents a new type of smart responsive materials with pressure-dependent photoluminescence (PL) changing properties, and emerging as potential candidates in polychromatic anticounterfeiting, chemical sensing, data processing, optical modulating, and so on[2]. However, traditional pressure-responsive PL materials based on organic or inorganic substances are usually suffering from low crystallinity, stability, sensibility, reversibility, tunability and controllability, thus severely limiting their practical applications[2a, 3]. In addition, the up to now reported pressure-induced fluorochromic materials are basically originated from one-photon excited fluorescence (1PEF) with such drawbacks of weak spatial confinement, limited penetration depth and low optical resolutions[4]. By contrast, two-photon excited fluorescence (2PEF) or multiphoton excited fluorescence (MPEF) materials undergo more complicated and fascinating processes in which two or more photons from a low energy radiation (usually in visible or NIR region) are absorbed by the material simultaneously, reaching the excited states, and emitting photoluminescence when transiting back to the ground state. As a result, MPEF materials are endowed with specific advantages including tight photonic focusing, deep tissue penetrating, less Rayleigh scattering, and low cell damaging, etc, which are beneficial for applications in high density data storage, long range telecommunication, high grade anticounterfeit, in vivo biological imaging, and so on[5]. It can be expected that, the combination of pressure-induced fluorochromic process and MPEF together in one single material might afford more advanced and intriguing photophysical properties with enticing applications.
Constructed by the integration of organic linkers and inorganic metal ions/clusters, photoluminescence metal-organic frameworks (PL-MOFs) can take advantage of the photoelectronic attributes from metals, ligands and guests included in the pores[6]. Moreover, PL-MOFs can be afforded with dynamic structures related with such factors as tunable density, porosity, interpenetration and component deformation, leading to multi-PL responsiveness[7]. However, up to now, pressure induced multiphoton fluorochromic MOFs have not been explored so far. Generally speaking, the following factors might be considered to construct a MOF with both multiphoton and pressure responsive PL properties. First, multiphoton absorption (MPA) and pressureinduced fluorochromic behavior can be inherited from certain organic chromophores. For example, TPE (tetraphenylethylene)- based molecules present typical chromophores to achieve the double roles. Second, the chromophores are linked by metal nodes/clusters into different topological structures and packing states. The resulted multivariate conformations and inter-/intramolecular environments for the chromophores will affect the pressure responsive fluorochromism and multiphoton behavior of the MOFs, such as the fluorescence color, quantum yield, pressure responsive fluorochromic sensitivity, MPA cross section and threshold, and so on[4d, 8]. Third, the pressure-related structural and fluorochromic transformations will also interfere with the multiphoton upconversion process and efficiency, and the PL thus obtained will bear signals from both sides.
Bearing the above considerations in mind, we systematically constructed and compared the pressure-induced multiphoton fluorochromism of five MOFs (LIFM-66, LIFM-114, LIFM-223, PCN-94 and PCN-128, LIFM stands for Lehn Institute of Functional Materials) from the same TPE (tetraphenylethylene)- based organic chromophore, namely, H4ETTC (4',4''',4''''',4'''''''- (ethene-1,1,2,2-tetrayl)tetrakis(([1,1'-biphenyl]-4-carboxylate)) and zirconium salt $(Z r O C l_{2}\cdot8H_{2}O$ or $Z r C l_{4}$ ). All the MOFs exhibit multiphoton excited fluorescence (MPEF) with pressure-induced PL color changing, and enhanced MPEF after pressure treatment, which are directly related with the topologies, flexibilities and packing densities of the frameworks. By this study, MPEF and pressure-induced fluorochromism is successfully coupled and correlated in MOFs.
## Results and Discussion
Pressure-induced 1PEF fluorochromism. Along with the above pressure tolerance study, distinct pressure responsive onephoton excited fluorochromism was observed for the five MOFs (Figures S31 & S32). As we can see, the eye-detectable color of all the samples under daylight changed from white or light green to yellow, while the PL emission color under one-photon UV radiation $(365\mathsf{n m})$ changed from blue (cyan) to greenish-yellow. In addition, the transformation processes are reversible by treating the pressed MOFs with trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) or benzoic acid (BA) at elevated temperature, except PCN-94 due to the rigidity of framework (Figures S31 & S32), and further certified by PXRD (Figures S18—S22). In order to get investigation into the mechanism of pressure-dependent photophysical properties of the five frameworks, solid-state UV-Vis absorption measurements for the H4ETTC ligand and the five MOFs before and after pressure were conducted using diffuse reflectance spectroscope (Figures S33 & S34). For which, H4ETTC ligand exhibits a broad absorption band from 290 to $600~\mathsf{n m}$ , while the five MOFs exhibit blue-shifted absorption basically below $450\mathsf{n m}$ . This manifests that the ETTC chromophores are segregated and restricted by linkage of $Z r_{6}$ -clusters in the coordination frameworks, and behave larger absorption bandgaps compared with the packing conformations in free ligand. While after pressure, the packing states among ETTC chromophores become denser. And therefore, the energy bandgaps of the frameworks are narrowed, showing absorption edges above $450\ \mathsf{n m}$ . As a result, the MOFs after pressure exhibit yellow color similar to H4ETTC ligand, while the pristine MOFs are white or light green color due to minor absorption in the visible region.
Pressure-induced 2PEF & 3PEF fluorochromism. Inspired by the observation of strong 1PEF behavior for the five MOFs, twophoton excited fluorescence (2PEF) was studied using a laser at 796 nm (Figures 2, S47—51). Remarkably, a series of strong 2PEF with the maximum positions ranging from 459 to $487~\mathsf{n m}$ were observed for pristine LIFM-66, LIFM-114, LIFM-223, PCN94 and PCN-128, respectively. In general, the 2PEF emission peaks of the five MOFs are similar with their corresponding 1PEF emissions, or with slight wavelength shifts owing to energy relaxation or re-absorption[12]. In order to verify the pressureinduced fluorochromic features of the five MOFs, 2PEF emission experiments were performed on the samples treated with a small laboratory compressing machine The PL changing tendency is identical with that observed in 1PEF experiments, for which the blue or cyan 2PEF emissions for pristine MOFs are gradually redshifted for the samples loaded with increasing pressure, and finally reaching yellow-green color.
Furthermore, we conducted 2PEF measurements for both pristine and compressed MOFs by applying different excitation power (Figures S52—82). The calculated n as defined by the slope of lg(PL intensity)-lg(excitation power) is near 2 for all the samples (Figures S83—93), confirming the second order absorption process for the emissions. 2PA thresholds for the samples were also obtained by determining the turning point on the PL intensity-excitation power curves. It is found that the samples have low threshold energies in the range of 22.7 to 123.8 $\upmu\up J$ . Generally speaking, dense framework and close packing arrangement of chromophoric linkers will favor MPEF response. This can be indeed proved by the pressure-induced fluorochromic 2PEF performance of the five MOFs. As shown in Figures 3a, 3b, and $\tt S94-103$ , LIFM-66 has the lowest framework and ETTC density, and therefore, its threshold energy is higher than the other MOFs. And due to pore-segmentation and density increase in LIFM-223 compared with PCN-128, its 2PA threshold is also reduced. This feature can be further improved in the compressed samples. As we can see, for all MOFs except PCN-94, lower 2PA thresholds will be obtained along with the emission red-shifts after treatment with pressure. Especially, a largely reduced 2PA threshold of $44.3~\upmu\mathrm{J}$ is obtained for the compressed LIFM-66 sample, almost one third that of the pristine LIFM-66 $(123.8~\upmu\mathsf{J})$ . And a lowest threshold energy of $22.7~\upmu\mathrm{J}$ is resulted for the compressed LIFM-114 among all samples, which is also less than half the value for the pristine LIFM-114. This clearly manifests that the framework contraction in the five MOFs by outer pressure stimulation will lead to both pressure-induced fluorochromism and 2PEF enhancement.
Within laser excitation wavelength from 740 to $960~\mathsf{n m}$ , we measured 2PA action cross sections2, see SI for detalils) for the pristine and pressed MOFs, which are in the range of 31–2217 GM with the maximum position at 780 or 800 nm. An obvious regularity can be found for the structure-2PA cross section relationship for the MOFs. As depicted in Figures 3c, 3d and S104, all MOF samples except LIFM-66 manifest higher 2PA cross sections than pure ${\mathsf{H}}_{4}{\mathsf{E T T C}}$ ligand, due to the packing and rigidifying effects of $Z r_{6}$ clusters’ connection in the coordination frameworks, showing advantages of MOFs to obtain superior 2PEF performance. In comparison, the open framework of LIFM66 with low ETTC density results in small 2PA cross section. Importantly, all the MOFs show increased 2PA cross section values after compression. Among which, the $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}$ for PCN-128 at the excitation of $780~\mathsf{n m}$ increased from 153 in pristine sample to 1897 GM in pressed sample, and the $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}$ for LIFM-114 increased from 210 to 2217 GM after pressure, more than ten folds larger. This is consistent with their structural transition extent. Typically, due to the breathing flexibility, the $c$ axis of LIFM-114 is decreased from 16.069 to $14.195\mathrm{~\AA~}$ after pressure (Table 1). This large degree of structural contraction is more significant compared with the other MOFs. And therefore, its increase of 2PA cross section is more effective by press manipulation. In comparison, the rigid framework of PCN-94 only shows minor increase in 2 value after press.
We then studied 3PEF properties of the materials to explore higher-order nonlinearity (Figures S105—123). For pristine samples, only LIFM-114, LIFM-223 and PCN-94 exhibit 3PEF emissions at $\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=1194\:\mathrm{nm}$ , while LIFM-66 and PCN-128 do not show such behavior due to low packing density. However, all the five MOFs present 3PEF emissions after pressure treatment, demonstrating that the formation of denser packing density is crucial for higher order nonlinear optical properties. Identical with 1PEF and 2PEF responses, 3PEF and piezofluoromerism attributes are coupled in the MOFs materials. As shown in Figure 3e, 3PEF emission wavelengths of the pristine MOFs are ranged from 475 to 485 nm in cyan region, while intensified 3PEF emissions for the pressed samples are observed in the range of 525 to $540~\mathsf{n m}$ in yellow region. The 3PA related processes for the samples can be further confirmed by the power dependent plots with slopes $\approx3$ .
Mechanism. From the above results, we proposed a general mechanism for the coupling of 1PEF, 2PEF, and 3PEF with pressure-induced fluorochromism in the five MOFs. As shown in Figure 3f, thanks to the heritage from dual-functional ETTC ligand, the MOFs can absorb $398\ \mathsf{n m\ U V}$ light via one-photon process, or NIR light at 796 or $1194\mathsf{n m}$ via two- or three-photon processes, respectively, reaching the excited $\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{n}}$ states. After energy relaxation and transiting back to the $\mathsf{s}_{0}$ ground state, corresponding 1PEF, 2PEF or 3PEF emissions in blue-cyan color are observed for the pristine MOF materials. Meanwhile, after the treatment of pressure, the framework lattices are contracted, resulting in conformational deformation of the ETTC chromophores. This will lead to enhanced inter- and intramolecular interactions between adjacent ETTCs and increased orbital overlaps. Therefore, pressure-induced fluorochromic phenomenon is happened, for which the $\mathsf{S}_{0}\to\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{n}}^{\mathsf{\Delta,}}$ energy gaps of the MOF materials become narrower compared with the pristine $\mathsf{S}_{0}\to\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{n}}$ states, and yellow-green colored 1PEF, 2PEF or 3PEF emissions with longer wavelengths are observed.
Moreover, both 1/2/3PEF and pressure-induced fluorochromic properties are exclusively dependent on the structural features and pressure-induced transformation degrees of the five MOFs. In general, larger framework densities and ETTC populations in pristine MOFs will lead to initial blue or cyan emissions with longer wavelength (for example, from the shortest 459 nm-1PEF in LIFM-66, to the longest 490 nm-1PEF in LIFM-114, and such is also the case for 2PEF and 3PEF emissions). Meanwhile, the ending emission wavelengths in the pressed MOFs are largely dependent on the framework flexibility and deformation abilities. For which, the rigid framework of PCN-94 can only undergo minor deformation within the pressure range of 0.6 Ton. Therefore, its ending emission wavelength is much shorter than the other four MOFs with relatively flexible frameworks. On the other hand, denser frameworks and larger ETTC populations also result in superior MPEF performance, such as larger TPA action cross sections and lower energy thresholds. And since pressure treatment will increase both framework and ETTC densities, the MPEF performance can be largely improved in the pressed MOFs.
And analyzing the structure-property relationships of the five MOFs herein studied, more targeted strategies might be put forward to gain optimized parameters for the above two attributes from the point of framework topology and flexibility designs (Figure 1). First, by virtue of pore segmentation using larger ETTC chromophore : $Z r_{6}$ cluster ratios, the csq-a network in PCN-128 can be densified into shp-a network in LIFM-223. As a result, both 1PEF quantum yield $(\Phi)$ and 2PA cross section $(\boldsymbol{\upeta}\upsigma_{2})$ for LIFM223 is increased compared with PCN-128. Second, for the aim of amplified pressure-induced fluorochromism switch between the pristine and pressed sample, an initial non-compact framework is favorable, since this will result in an initial emission with shorter wavelength. Such will be the case for PCN-128 and LIFM-66 compared with the other three MOFs. Third, to gain more significant increase of 2PA cross section for the MOFs by pressure treatment, a more flexible or breathing-type framework might be effective, such as the case in LIFM-114 and PCN-128. In this way, efficient coupling and associating performances of 1/2/3PEF and pressure-induced fluorochromism are correlated in metal-organic frameworks for the first time, as far as we know.
## Conclusion
In conclusion, we systematically studied the pressureinduced one-photon, two-photon, and three-photon excited fluorochromic properties of five Zr-MOFs from the same ETTC linkers before and after pressure treatment. Both the 1/2/3PEF and fluorochromic performances of the MOFs are directly correlated with their structural features and pressure-induced deformations. And improved MPEF properties can be obtained for the samples after pressure treatment, due to closer packing density and enhanced inter-/intra-molecular interactions of the ETTC chromophores in the MOFs. Among which, the pressed LIFM-114 sample with the highest packing density shows the lowest 2PA threshold $(22.7~\upmu\mathsf{J})$ and largest 2PA cross section (2217 GM) with more than ten folds increment from the pristine to the pressed samples. This study opens up an avenue for further design and application of multifunctional MOFs with both pressure-induced fluorochromism and multi-photon responses, and finding practical applications in such areas as composite optical storage, in vivo pressure sensing and imaging, confidential anti-counterfeiting and barcoding, and so on.
